WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
[upbeat music]

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.450
- I am always so pleased to talk to Jony.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:27.260
He is of course to the design world,

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.860
either an oracle, or a God,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.890
someone whose projects,

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.000
once as chief design officer for Apple,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.150
now, as the founder of the
design collective LoveFrom,

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:44.163
a breathlessly awaited
and inspire a kind of awe.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.290
He's also famously private
and rarely heard from

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.090
which only adds to his mystique.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.270
To his friends though

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.790
and I am so proud to count
myself as one of them,

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.290
Jony has such warmth and
generosity and intelligence.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.220
He is, I'd say a humanist,

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:07.310
a delight to talk to, a wonderful father,

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:11.080
and loving husband to his
extraordinary wife, Heather,

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.410
someone who cares about
people as much as he cares

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:18.320
about the extraordinary
things that he designs.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.390
The products he's made, the
iPhone, the Apple Watch,

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.990
we all know the list by now are brilliant,

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.650
precisely because they have a human scale,

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.180
and an intuitive simplicity and purity,

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.663
that make them feel essential.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.780
They're tools, but at
this point they feel more

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.570
like natural complements to
the busy lives that we lead.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.610
And of course,

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.983
none of us know what we
would do without them.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.750
So it's been two years
since Jony left Apple.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.470
And I know that we're
also excited to hear about

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:57.610
the turns his life and career
have taken with LoveFrom,

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.430
which collaborates not only with Apple,

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:04.490
but with brands like Ferrari and Airbnb.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.610
And as so much

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.290
of LoveFrom's work revolves
around climate change,

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:13.070
I'm especially eager to
hear his thoughts on this.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:17.770
Please join me in welcoming, Sir Jony Ive.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.980
Jony, nice to see you again
back at a WIRED conference.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.090
I think it's about four
years since we talked last,

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.183
well, at a WIRED conference.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.480
And these past few weeks
have marked several,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.180
big anniversaries for you.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.480
And so, today I wanted to
ask you about some of them.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.480
For starters, 20 years
ago, iPod was launched.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.890
You spent most of your career
creating devices we hold

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:48.030
in our hands or on our desk.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:52.130
And then you moved to devices,
we wear like the Apple Watch.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.633
What was the biggest challenge
in the shift to wearables,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.683
and where on earth are all
these devices gonna from here?

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.553
- Well, it's certainly been,

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:10.490
I can't quite remember a few weeks,

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.853
that have been quite
so loaded in terms of,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.821
important anniversaries
that have given me,

00:03:18.821 --> 00:03:20.671
[indistinct] to think about the past.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.750
These haven't been those sort

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.360
of dreadfully polite,
gentle reminiscences,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.810
that these have been,

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:31.483
I think really fairly loaded.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:38.550
The iPod, I struggle to really
get my head around that.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.630
It is truly 20 years.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:42.930
I think iPod marked

00:03:44.122 --> 00:03:47.313
the beginning of a very
new and important chapter,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.933
for Apple, or as it was
pre-iPod Apple computer.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.170
So up until that point in time,

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:01.580
Apple made general
purpose computing devices.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.500
And one of the powers,

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.930
I think one of the
things that was so unique

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.180
and special I think
about the computer was,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.510
its ability to be general purpose.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:16.440
And I think what the
iPod really marked was

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.510
the beginning of creating,

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.970
far more specific products and devices.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.880
Now it happened at a time
I think of extraordinary,

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:32.560
I think, both opportunity and need.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.860
So, there were a number of
technological affordances

00:04:37.815 --> 00:04:39.750
that sort of presented themselves,

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:44.313
in terms of storing lots of
data in small physical spaces.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:50.300
But I think the need was that
there was almost this sort

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.500
of existential question,
about the value of music.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.160
This is 20 years ago,

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.453
when people realized they
didn't have to pay for music.

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:04.693
And so I think that was
a context or a backdrop,

00:05:06.065 --> 00:05:06.920
for doing something,

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:08.910
creating something that
was quite important.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:12.610
And I think, the iPod,

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.283
really represents that marriage of,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.730
I think, innovative and great hardware,

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.770
software and services.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.580
And from a design perspective,

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.630
we thought the opportunities were,

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:32.460
I think, exciting, compelling, but also,

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.070
this seemed important.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:37.563
And so, from a design point of view,

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.840
the context back, you know, 20 years ago,

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.673
consumer electronics tended
to be plastic objects

00:05:49.032 --> 00:05:51.449
that were really represented.

00:05:53.836 --> 00:05:56.570
I know, I mean, they tended
to be fairly sort of arbitrary

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:58.710
in terms of their form.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:00.850
And we genuinely,

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:03.340
we honestly felt that there was something

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:07.670
so profoundly important about music.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.980
And so we wanted to develop a design,

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:14.260
that somehow acknowledged that gravitas,

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:17.220
that this was not another
piece of you know,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.453
random consumer electronics.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.010
And so I think that started
with first of all treating it

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.450
as a single system.

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:28.220
So, you know, the
headphones were, you know,

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.580
we thought about them
in terms of being part

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:34.333
of the same product, being
the actual player device.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:40.450
We developed a new sort of
palette of materials that range

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:45.820
from double shop plastic but
to polished stainless steel

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:47.353
that was laser edge.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:52.587
We did a ton of work on
not only the materials

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.790
but also just on the color.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:58.080
I mean, now it seems obvious,

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.513
with the benefit of
hindsight it seems obvious,

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:03.910
to, you know, that they're
headphones would be white.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.253
I remember 20 years ago,

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.790
the sort of shock and
resistance to, you know,

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:14.323
why would you possibly, call
attention to these headphones,

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.900
that where I saw is like
these little white flags,

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.260
that spoke to, you know, the
one visible part of the system.

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:26.843
So, I mean, the other
thing I think was that,

00:07:28.433 --> 00:07:31.540
we wanted to try and create a design,

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:36.240
that was so distinctive, that
it didn't require a logo,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.570
and a name on the front,
which is unheard of.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:44.065
20 years ago in consumer electronics,

00:07:44.065 --> 00:07:48.180
you couldn't move for, you
know, shouting about, you know,

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.400
logo and the company that made it.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.600
And, I think what happened,
and this happens occasionally,

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.930
it doesn't happen very
often but sometimes,

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:01.680
a design can assume as you know,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.540
a sort of a momentum where,

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.143
it assumes a sort of a cultural,

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:14.653
I don't know, gravity and significance,

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.860
and what we started to hear
about it referred to as,

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:19.973
as an icon.

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:22.700
But this did mark,

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.410
I think the beginning of moving away

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.610
from what was general purpose,

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:32.610
to something that was more
specific in what it did,

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:36.050
and more specific in who it did it for.

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:38.740
So it was a personal device,

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:40.350
that was, I mean, we started

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:43.093
to see people wearing our products.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:48.940
- I always remember thinking
about logos and icons.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:51.330
I always remember the first
time I came to visit you

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.340
in your incredible headquarters at Apple.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:55.850
And you were adamant,

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:58.050
that there shouldn't be a shop,

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.140
that you didn't want to have a shop,

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:02.260
but that you said that you had realized

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.800
that actually everybody wanted to buy,

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.440
that coffee mug with the Apple logo.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.350
So what did you ever do about the shop?

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.350
Did you have it in the end or not?

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:14.820
- No, we do.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:15.810
I mean, Apple Park,

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:19.610
there is actually a
fantastic visitor center.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:21.790
And as part of the visitor center,

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:24.820
you can buy your mugs
with the Apple logo on.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.610
- Yeah but I seem to
remember you were very,

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:29.240
very against it.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.660
So, obviously that was 20 years ago.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:36.510
If we look forward to
20 years from now, Jony,

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:39.280
what do you think
wearable devices will be?

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:41.733
What do you think the future is for them?

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:47.130
- Well, I think what really,

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:51.143
I mean in many ways, I
think Apple's journey began,

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.303
in the '70s to make,

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.113
what was incredibly powerful
technology personal,

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:04.020
with the personal computer.

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:09.020
And I think that trajectory,
that journey, started then.

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:12.110
And then now I think there
are these salient moments

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.110
along the way,

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:18.260
that mark the capability,

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:23.260
the technology becoming more
specific, and more personal.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:27.980
So I think the iPod was a
terribly important marker

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:29.423
on that journey.

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:35.913
I think another significant
one was the Apple Watch.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:41.770
So as the technology
becomes more powerful,

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:45.520
but also physically becomes smaller,

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:48.140
and that it is possible for it to be worn,

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.363
worn simply and worn easily,

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:56.480
the products become clearly
not only more personal,

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.060
but they become more intimate.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:01.240
And I think, I mean, there's no doubt.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:04.063
And I know you and I have
talked about this in the past.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:10.860
Without question, some
of those products will,

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:12.863
disappear beneath our skin.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.660
I can't think of anything
more personal, more specific,

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:19.090
more individual, and more intimate

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:21.553
than things being insiders.

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.200
But, that's not gonna be all

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.450
of the products obviously.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:29.653
I mean, while we remain physical
beings and while we remain,

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:34.830
in a physical world
relating to one another,

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:37.520
while we have our eyes and our hands,

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:41.680
we will also want to
relate beyond ourselves.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:42.513
And so there's,

00:11:42.513 --> 00:11:45.940
I think going to be a categories
and categories of products,

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:48.370
that while I think they
will be more intimate

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:49.663
and more personal,

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:55.530
but they will be worn,
outside of our skin.

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.210
- Right, right.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.983
Well, we also talked a lot
about how we could make,

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:03.213
the Apple Watch smaller.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:08.063
That was something you
were really challenged by.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:12.580
That's something that I
would love to see one day.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:14.653
But 10 years ago Jony,

00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:19.510
the world lost your
creative partner Steve Jobs,

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.590
who you referred to as

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:24.420
the most inquisitive
human I have ever met.

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:27.290
And I read that amazing
tribute that you wrote

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:30.020
to him recently in the
Wall Street Journal.

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:34.010
So, what do you miss most about him,

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:36.793
the conversations, the
person, the creativity?

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:44.853
- Yeah, it's been an
interesting process and I mean,

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.680
I'm sure it's one that
many of us can relate to.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.970
I think perhaps rather naively,

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:58.283
I thought my sense of Steve,

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:02.960
when I remember him and think about him

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.773
which is pretty much every day.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:10.903
I thought my sense of
him would remain somehow,

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:14.770
inviolate and stable.

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:19.770
And, what's surprised me,
is as I think about him,

00:13:21.672 --> 00:13:22.505
my sense of him,

00:13:22.505 --> 00:13:26.950
my sense of what his
contribution truly was,

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.870
has grown and has evolved.

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.430
And I think that's a comment
on a number of things.

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:33.553
I think not least,

00:13:36.211 --> 00:13:38.340
sort of the current situation and context

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:41.430
that we've been living
through in the last few years.

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:46.240
But I think where I have seen
this shift in my own sense

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:49.173
and my own remembering of him,

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:54.900
was less about his achievement,

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:59.900
less about his, you know,
the products of his process,

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:06.380
but it's become more and more about,

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:10.320
how he worked and the
values and the priorities,

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:12.270
that he had in his working.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:15.263
And so, now as I described,

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.873
I've never met anybody more truly curious,

00:14:22.099 --> 00:14:24.693
more inquisitive than Steve.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:27.410
I mean, those are easy words

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:29.040
and they can be used fairly glibly

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:34.040
but he was constantly questioning.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.963
He wasn't defined by anybody else's dogma.

00:14:38.219 --> 00:14:43.219
There was this relish, this
celebration of being surprised,

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:47.320
even if the surprise actually
meant that he was wrong,

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:50.230
he was far more interested
in the learning,

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:52.070
than being right.

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:54.893
And it was just that love,

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:59.240
and that almost childlike wonder,

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:02.183
of discovering and questioning.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:04.423
But I think,

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:09.700
with some space I realize,

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:13.980
and this has influenced me so very much.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.810
I think it's very easy to think.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:20.280
Well, you know, that can just
be an innate personality trait

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:21.970
to be curious.

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:25.940
And, over the last sort of decade or so,

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:28.540
I've become increasingly aware,

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:32.810
of actually just how much
intention is required,

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:36.513
if you don't want to just fit in to,

00:15:38.553 --> 00:15:40.470
I guess the status quo or just fit

00:15:41.388 --> 00:15:44.093
in a nice, polite, convenient way,

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:47.883
that large groups of people tend to,

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:50.850
sort of push you towards.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:54.760
And I've become more and
more aware of the intention,

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:59.760
the resolve, and the
determination, to be truly curious,

00:15:59.850 --> 00:16:03.360
to be truly, you know, questioning.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:05.960
I think what happens,

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:08.423
is that when we're in a group of people,

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:13.130
we tend to want to talk
about what's known,

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:16.533
because it's much easier to
have a polite conversation.

00:16:18.715 --> 00:16:22.260
Most people I think don't
relish confrontation

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:24.183
and they want to establish,

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:28.110
some sort of connection with other people.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.870
And that's very hard
to do in a large group,

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:35.630
if what you're talking about
is partial and tentative.

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:39.743
If it's just a nebulous, fragile idea,

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:43.840
that's a much more difficult conversation,

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:45.590
than talking about the problem,

00:16:45.590 --> 00:16:47.870
because I think the thing with problems,

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:51.613
by definition a problem is understood.

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:54.670
And so of course people want to talk about

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:56.390
what they understand.

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:59.400
And so generally the
problem consumes the oxygen.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:02.010
It takes all of the attention.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:04.940
And, I am more and more struck

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:08.803
by how much energy and effort,

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:15.430
that he put in to encouraging people,

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:17.640
to be comfortable talking about

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:19.800
what's tentative and partial.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:20.750
And that's ideas.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:25.750
And his just innate respect to reverence

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:32.283
for the creative process
was extraordinary.

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:35.093
And I don't know,

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:40.067
the older I get, the more important,

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:45.040
both the contribution, and I don't know,

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:49.293
the sort of, the moral
imperative around creating,

00:17:50.227 --> 00:17:52.750
you know, personally seems to be the case.

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:57.750
And so, yeah, I am particularly mindful

00:18:01.537 --> 00:18:05.470
of just his attitudes and his thinking,

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:09.243
perhaps more than the
predictable achievements.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:13.940
- Listening to you Jony,

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:18.920
I'm wondering if these
thoughts were in your mind

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:23.920
when you left Apple and formed LoveFrom.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:29.160
And I'd love to understand
what made you choose that name.

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:31.020
And could you tell us,

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:32.960
I know a lot of it, you can't tell us,

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:35.660
but can you tell us more
about the motivation

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:38.200
and the people behind the company?

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.070
And I believe an enormous
amount of the work

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:44.380
that you're doing now is
connected to climate change.

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:49.380
But, how much of it was spurred
by all those extraordinary,

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:57.940
moving descriptions you
just gave us of Steve?

00:18:57.940 --> 00:18:59.770
Were they connected to the decision

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:01.563
to create this company?

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:05.500
- Now, I think, I mean,
that's a great question.

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:10.500
I think, of course it's always
a question of when, not if.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:15.550
And so, the timing felt right

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:20.670
for me to change the nature
of my relationship with Apple.

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:24.230
I still work very closely
with my friends at Apple.

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:29.230
They're one of the collaborators
that we're working with.

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:35.110
But yes, without a doubt,

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:38.483
I mean, in terms of
where the name came from,

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:44.363
I was always struck by how
often we talk about what we do,

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:49.803
and how we do it, but we
don't often talk about why.

00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:53.980
And I'm personally, when I'm trying to,

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:55.890
whether it's me interviewing

00:19:55.890 --> 00:19:58.250
or it's I'm trying to establish,

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:01.210
some sort of sense of a
relationship with somebody,

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:04.813
I'm always personally intrigued
by what their motivation is.

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:10.850
And, I will never forget
Steve describing the why.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:16.260
And, he said that, you know,
when you make something,

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:20.203
when you create something
and you do it with care,

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:23.840
and you do it with love,

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:27.840
you may never meet the person,

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:32.720
that is going to use what you've made.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:36.360
You may never know
their name, their story.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:38.690
You probably won't even shake their hand.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:43.690
But, if you create with
a genuine care and love,

00:20:46.546 --> 00:20:49.320
and I love the way he expressed this,

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:54.320
it's a way of expressing our
gratitude for the species.

00:20:55.690 --> 00:20:58.860
And I just thought that

00:21:00.669 --> 00:21:05.440
the profoundly important
nature of our motivation,

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:07.130
would be a good starting place

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:10.010
for a description of who we are.

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:14.333
And so that's where the name
LoveFrom really came from.

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.370
The company, I think is an unusual one

00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:22.450
because it really is
just the very, you know,

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:25.200
it's the gentle gathering of,

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:28.370
I think some extraordinary creatives.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:33.370
And it's a diverse group of creatives.

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:38.363
You know, we're designers,
architects, engineers, writers,

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:42.923
mathematicians, musicians, filmmakers.

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:48.050
The thing that seems to connect us,

00:21:48.050 --> 00:21:50.253
I think is that we are,

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:53.070
you know, we may be known by the work

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:54.833
that we've done in the past,

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:59.680
but I think that we are
genuinely connected and united

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:03.020
because we believe that
our most important work,

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:06.893
our most important
contribution, is in the future.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:13.070
And there is a real
passion and belief there,

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:15.050
but also a real passion and belief

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:18.750
in the value of collaborating,

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:23.453
with a diverse group of creatives.

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:29.880
Our work really sort of
falls into three categories.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:33.543
I mean, we have one category
of work where we're working,

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:38.783
very close to founders, leaders,

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:44.630
in a sort of a multi-year relationship.

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:49.630
So, you know, that's Airbnb,
Apple, Amazon collective.

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:55.440
We just recently announced Ferrari.

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:57.650
But what's exciting
about these relationships

00:22:57.650 --> 00:23:00.090
is they're more about a relationship

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:04.043
that transcends individual
projects but they are longterm.

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:08.150
And I think the value there is,

00:23:08.150 --> 00:23:09.610
the right project

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:12.973
in five years time
isn't obvious right now.

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:18.120
And so, and I've always loved,
you know, that as a context

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:21.863
which is a deep relationship
with a group of people.

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:27.830
The second category of work and projects

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:29.630
that we're involved in,

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:33.870
really is purely for the
love, purely for the joy,

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:36.823
purely for the belief
that we can be useful.

00:23:39.580 --> 00:23:44.210
And that's very project based,
and is incredibly diverse.

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:48.310
I mean, it ranges from
designing urban garden systems,

00:23:48.310 --> 00:23:51.823
to working with friends
at, for example, Moncler.

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:53.950
It involves us working

00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:57.393
with his Royal Highness Prince
Charles on the Terra Carta.

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:02.650
And there are sort of half a
dozen projects we're working on

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:06.468
which I would love to talk
about irritating the icon.

00:24:06.468 --> 00:24:09.850
- [indistinct] have a love from
puffer jacket from Moncler?

00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:11.803
- Oh, that was just the beginning.

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:16.273
I mean, as you said,

00:24:17.660 --> 00:24:22.010
with huge intention
almost without exception,

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:27.010
all of our work is either
very directly or indirectly,

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:31.190
but all of our work
almost without exception,

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:35.450
is trying to make a
contribution to the crisis

00:24:35.450 --> 00:24:40.163
that we're facing,
relative to climate change.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:47.100
And I think that this little
group that we've assembled,

00:24:50.200 --> 00:24:51.640
we are hoping that we can be

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:54.380
in some way useful in a
number of different ways

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:58.020
but we are excited about being useful,

00:24:58.020 --> 00:25:00.513
and joining what is clearly a,

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:04.060
I've been very struck by

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:08.260
the diverse collection of people

00:25:08.260 --> 00:25:10.373
who have made this their life's work.

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:15.133
- So you're a collective,
you're a collective?

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:21.050
- And you know, some of my dear friends

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:24.060
who I'm lucky enough to be working with.

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:26.410
But the collective ranges from

00:25:28.090 --> 00:25:30.000
the legend that is [indistinct]

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.173
to the legend that is Peter Saville.

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:35.330
But as I said,

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:39.533
there is a sort of a
serenity or calm in that,

00:25:42.340 --> 00:25:45.350
you know, I think many of us
are known by what we have done

00:25:45.350 --> 00:25:50.230
and we are much more interested
though, in what we could do.

00:25:50.230 --> 00:25:52.660
And I think we do believe that we can do,

00:25:52.660 --> 00:25:57.570
so much more particularly
if we are working together.

00:25:57.570 --> 00:25:58.810
- Together, yeah.

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:01.830
Well, I think it's been
such an interesting,

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:04.010
past couple of years Jony

00:26:04.010 --> 00:26:07.180
and everybody reevaluating,

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:09.180
what it is they wanna do with their lives,

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:14.060
what they want to do for
other people around them.

00:26:14.060 --> 00:26:15.940
And I'm just,

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:20.260
obviously you thought about
LoveFrom before COVID.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:25.260
But, has COVID hastened your
desire to put your work out

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:28.120
into the world?

00:26:28.120 --> 00:26:31.360
Has it changed the values
that you had in your mind

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:33.000
when you started it?

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:35.850
I mean, we've had people,

00:26:35.850 --> 00:26:37.250
I think I was reading that two

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:40.360
and a half million people
quit their jobs in August,

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:41.350
just because they wanted

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:43.600
to do something completely different.

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:45.390
Obviously you are ahead of the curve

00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:48.890
but I'm wondering as we emerge from COVID,

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:52.893
what is it that's top of your mind as you,

00:26:53.910 --> 00:26:56.050
start to think about all
this extraordinary work

00:26:56.050 --> 00:26:57.143
that you'll be doing?

00:26:59.240 --> 00:27:03.630
- Now I mean, that's a wonderful question.

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:05.343
It's a very big question.

00:27:08.350 --> 00:27:10.300
I think the last sort of year and a half

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:11.550
that we've lived through,

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:15.300
I think highlights,

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:19.863
some fairly significant challenges for us.

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:23.693
I do think I've been
interested for a long time,

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:26.683
in the relationship where, you know,

00:27:26.683 --> 00:27:29.820
between what is digital virtual

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:32.800
and what is material and tangible.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:35.573
I mean, it's interesting
the first shipping product,

00:27:38.466 --> 00:27:41.980
from LoveFrom, very intentionally

00:27:41.980 --> 00:27:45.343
but perhaps rather
surprisingly is a typeface.

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:48.483
And we developed a typeface.

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:52.650
I mean, we've been
working on this typeface,

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:55.000
fanatically and with such care and love

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:57.250
but for over two years,

00:27:57.250 --> 00:28:00.433
but it's a typeface based on work that,

00:28:01.650 --> 00:28:06.650
a typographer John Baskerville
developed over 200 years ago.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:10.330
And we managed to track down,

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:15.330
the original steel punches from the 1700s.

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:20.720
And we scanned and redrew
all of the characters.

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:25.380
The type set now has over,

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:30.380
I think it's over 7,000
distinct characters, symbols,

00:28:32.150 --> 00:28:34.883
and a range of different styles.

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:38.760
But what's been so interesting,

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:43.760
is that I think it's a lovely
comment on our huge respect,

00:28:48.550 --> 00:28:51.700
and deference to the traditions

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:54.700
and the excellence of the past,

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:56.540
but combining that with a belief

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:58.393
about the importance of the future.

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:02.940
And I also think it's a nice comment,

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:05.610
on our struggles and preoccupation between

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:06.800
the physical world,

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:10.270
between those 200 old steel punches,

00:29:10.270 --> 00:29:12.670
and the design of a typeface,

00:29:12.670 --> 00:29:17.670
that is also infinitely
controllable in terms

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:22.840
of varying optical sizes,
weights, in the digital world.

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:29.140
So, I think one of the
things that has so struck me,

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:34.760
I mean, one of many, but has
been the slight confusion.

00:29:38.010 --> 00:29:40.703
And of course the anxiety
that's consequent,

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:46.150
as we struggle to understand
what is virtual and digital

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:47.653
and what is material.

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:52.980
And as you know, I mean, I've always been,

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:57.980
like I think many
designers, applied artists,

00:29:58.220 --> 00:30:02.320
I've always been a keen
student of process.

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:07.010
I've always spent a lot of
time looking at how I work.

00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:10.670
I mean, in some ways it's very
selfish, it's for my sanity,

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:13.760
to be able to cope with those times when,

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:17.433
ideas neither come, or
they're just appalling.

00:30:19.957 --> 00:30:23.130
But one of the things
that has become so clear,

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:25.730
while I am so grateful

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:30.470
for our virtual and digital communication,

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:35.470
the danger is it's now
so good in so many ways,

00:30:36.370 --> 00:30:38.270
that we make that dreadful mistake,

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:40.800
of thinking that it is
in some way a surrogate

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:43.840
for actually being together in person.

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:46.223
And as a creative person,

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:51.520
you know, those sorts of very
transactional tactical ways

00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:55.500
of being together are great for
a whole bunch of activities.

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:59.260
But to try and do something
new, to create something new,

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:01.343
to think in a different way,

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:06.100
it's really quite challenging, isn't it?

00:31:06.100 --> 00:31:10.010
I mean, I think a huge part
of the creative process,

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:13.133
a big component of being
curious and inquisitive,

00:31:14.323 --> 00:31:17.703
is learning to actually listen to ignore.

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:22.963
- You were talking about the
quietest voice in the room.

00:31:24.380 --> 00:31:28.370
- It's so funny, I mean,
if you're on, you know,

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:30.350
a virtual video call,

00:31:30.350 --> 00:31:33.470
and you're trying to
encourage people to listen,

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:34.680
and everybody's quiet,

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:37.250
we assume there's an audio problem.

00:31:37.250 --> 00:31:42.250
So, to just be quiet
and to enjoy being quiet

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:46.110
and to listening and to think,

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:49.290
you know, video connections like these,

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:53.943
do not encourage us to
be quiet and thoughtful.

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:56.393
And I think, you know,

00:31:56.393 --> 00:32:00.050
going back to my recollections
and thinking about Steve,

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:03.160
some of the most important times we spent,

00:32:03.160 --> 00:32:05.070
were probably on walks together

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:08.050
when neither of us said very much,

00:32:08.050 --> 00:32:11.520
but we were thinking in
proximity to each other.

00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:12.353
- Yes.

00:32:12.353 --> 00:32:15.370
- And that's the beauty
of ideas, isn't it?

00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:19.060
That they can come very quietly.

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:22.370
I think almost always in my experience,

00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:26.110
the most powerful ideas do occur quietly,

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:28.690
and they are fragile,

00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:33.690
and they need to be with
reverence and respect,

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:38.340
gently cared for as they
evolve to become powerful.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:41.730
And I've always loved that
sort of strange disconnect,

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:46.730
between something that eventually
can be so, so powerful,

00:32:47.510 --> 00:32:50.083
but started so gently.

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:54.173
And in such a sort of a
tentative partial way.

00:32:56.500 --> 00:33:00.920
- Jony, you are an extraordinary father,

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:04.823
and you have the most
extraordinary two boys that,

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:09.240
really accredit to you and to Heather.

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:10.650
And I wonder,

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:13.860
when you think of the kind of world

00:33:13.860 --> 00:33:16.130
that they're going to inherit,

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:17.120
how do you think

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:20.370
about making it a more
hopeful place for them?

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:24.570
Because, I know their future
is so important to you.

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:26.193
- Well, thank you first of all.

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:33.750
Yeah, I mean, I hope the
world is in one piece

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:38.673
as they grow up.

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:42.700
They're both absurdly creative.

00:33:42.700 --> 00:33:45.470
And as you know a
consequence of that practice,

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:50.373
is that you have to be
optimistic and faithful.

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:56.083
And so they have such an affirming,

00:33:57.940 --> 00:34:01.070
and very positive view of the future.

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:02.430
I think, you know,

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:05.940
sometimes I have to bite
down and keep my, you know,

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:08.390
how consumed I am with anxiety.

00:34:08.390 --> 00:34:10.763
I try and hide that from them.

00:34:12.790 --> 00:34:17.790
I hope, that there is a way
that we can regard and place,

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:28.173
these very powerful technologies
and tools that we have.

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:32.840
I hope we can find the
right place for them,

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:36.260
in our personal lives and
in our collective lives.

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:39.480
I think that this is not a new challenge.

00:34:39.480 --> 00:34:42.820
I mean, those tools that
are the most powerful,

00:34:42.820 --> 00:34:47.360
that I believe have the
potential to be the most helpful.

00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:50.800
So often we look at history,

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:52.963
they can be the most destructive.

00:34:53.930 --> 00:34:57.280
And I think one of the challenges
that we have at the moment

00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:00.230
is that we are moving so quickly.

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:05.230
I think we are struggling to
comprehend, to understand,

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:10.990
both the tremendous power of these tools.

00:35:10.990 --> 00:35:15.990
And we are struggling to
understand the responsibility,

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:21.540
and perhaps the frameworks
that we need to create,

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:24.360
to try and encourage these tools

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:26.930
and opportunities to be used usefully,

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:29.500
and to try and reduce

00:35:29.500 --> 00:35:33.003
the instances of them being
used in a negative way.

00:35:34.090 --> 00:35:37.460
So I think I'm hugely
encouraged that the tools

00:35:37.460 --> 00:35:41.860
and opportunities are there to help us,

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:44.500
it's just our attitudes towards them.

00:35:44.500 --> 00:35:49.500
But I do hope, that the
world the boys grow into,

00:35:52.420 --> 00:35:57.333
I hope there's just a little
bit more surprise and wonder,

00:35:58.680 --> 00:36:02.573
and just an awful lot less cynical dogma.

00:36:03.980 --> 00:36:08.980
I hope it's a world where there
is just a natural enthusiasm

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:12.950
and interest in ideas,

00:36:12.950 --> 00:36:15.370
and there is more of a rejection

00:36:15.370 --> 00:36:20.370
of just yet another opinion
masquerading as fact,

00:36:21.440 --> 00:36:25.533
or as truth, or as even
as useful as an idea.

00:36:26.950 --> 00:36:30.647
But again, I go back to that behavior

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:36.720
that we often say is childlike.

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:40.363
And I mean, our children are curious,

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:45.590
they're inquisitive,
and they want to learn,

00:36:45.590 --> 00:36:48.740
and we've gotta figure
out a way of educate,

00:36:48.740 --> 00:36:53.350
you know, not having that
lovely predisposition

00:36:53.350 --> 00:36:56.463
to be curious and inquisitive,
educated out of them.

00:36:58.360 --> 00:37:01.730
- Well, Johnny, I'm sure
that their lives are filled

00:37:01.730 --> 00:37:03.470
with surprise and wonder.

00:37:03.470 --> 00:37:07.120
And we also look forward to seeing all

00:37:07.120 --> 00:37:10.783
the magical things you're
gonna create from LoveFrom.

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:15.290
I'm especially excited
about my Moncler jacket,

00:37:15.290 --> 00:37:17.070
and more things to come.

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:21.113
And I love you, I miss
you, I'll see you soon.

00:37:21.113 --> 00:37:23.610
- I'll see you soon Anna, lots of love.

00:37:23.610 --> 00:37:25.369
Thank you so much.

00:37:25.369 --> 00:37:27.952
[upbeat music]

